record raw-autotune.evidence.archive-speed-tree-clean-20260826/1 : evidence {
    title "Tests, clippy, fmt and check clean on Linux for the archive-speed and metadata work"
    state verified
    result pass
    method command
    observed_at git:2ce6bf6210815faf878df448047f7621cb9903cd
    command "cargo test --release; cargo clippy --all-targets --release; cargo fmt --check; cargo check --all-targets"
    summary """
        Linux x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, working tree at 2ce6bf62 plus the archive-speed change.

        cargo test --release: 362 passed, 0 failed, across the library (351) and the four integration binaries. cargo clippy --all-targets --release: no warnings. cargo fmt --check: clean. cargo check --all-targets: clean, no warnings.

        The new coverage is three tests. raw_highlight::the_spatial_floor_declines_sparse_clipping_and_reports_current pins that a below-floor frame is untouched and reports Current, that a frame clearing the floor reports the requested method, and that floor 0 produces the same samples as a frame that cleared the floor. metadata::the_rec2020_profile_is_a_distinct_valid_profile_and_srgb_is_unmoved and metadata::output_colour_space_is_srgb_for_every_working_space pin the generalized ICC builder and today's colour-space answer. tests/in_memory_api::the_in_memory_render_carries_exif_icc_and_an_explicit_colour_space renders a real ProShot DNG and checks the EXIF blob equals SourceMetadata::exif_payload at the rendered dimensions, carries no Exif NUL NUL header, parses standalone with orientation normalized, that the ICC is the cached sRGB profile, and that metadata false returns None for both while leaving every pixel identical.

        What was deliberately not run: the full 376-file field-by-field --dry-run --summary regression diff against a previous-release binary. The change moves default output on frames below the floor by design, so an identity diff is the wrong instrument; the byte-identity properties that are invariant were checked directly instead and are recorded separately.
        """
}

record raw-autotune.evidence.backlit-foliage-glow-is-exposure-20260810/1 : evidence {
    title "Backlit-foliage glow on _DSC1289 is captured flare shown by exposure, not a render halo"
    state superseded
    result pass
    method command
    observed_at git:65c476863f2f22acaee9df87bdd656b52012464a
    command "raw-autotune raw/raw_3rd_batch/_DSC1289.ARW --dump-stages ... ; ablations over --highlight-reconstruction 0, --sharpen 0, --chroma-denoise 0, --exposure-bias -1"
    summary """
        The bright glow around foliage backlit by sky on _DSC1289 is not produced by any render stage. Foliage 1-3px from sky renders at 130.14 versus 102.12 at 12-25px, and that gradient is unchanged by highlight reconstruction (128.93 near with strength 0), sharpening, or chroma denoise. The near/far ratio is 1.261 in the scene-linear dump and 1.282 after the tone curve, so the curve does not create it either: the glow is veiling flare and demosaic mixing already present in the captured data. At matched 1456px scale the relative glow is smaller than Windows Photo Viewer's own render of the same ARW (ratio 1.144 versus 1.172); what differs is absolute foliage brightness, 72.67 versus 39.09. Rendering the same frame at --exposure-bias -1 gives foliage 47.80 at ratio 1.151. The visible defect is therefore the automatic exposure decision (+1.04 EV) making real flare conspicuous, not a halo-producing operator. Note the embedded preview oracle measured key -0.34 EV for this frame.
        """
}

record raw-autotune.evidence.backlit-foliage-glow-is-exposure-20260810/2 : evidence {
    title "Backlit-foliage glow on _DSC1289 is captured flare shown by midtone contrast, not exposure (key name is legacy)"
    state verified
    result pass
    method command
    observed_at git:65c476863f2f22acaee9df87bdd656b52012464a
    command """
        raw-autotune raw/raw_3rd_batch/_DSC1289.ARW --dump-stages ... ; ablations over --highlight-reconstruction 0, --sharpen 0, --chroma-denoise 0, --exposure-bias -1
        """
    summary """
        CORRECTION of rev 1, whose key name and conclusion both said exposure. The glow is not produced by any render stage and is not an exposure error. Foliage 1-3px from sky renders at 130.14 against 102.12 at 12-25px, unchanged by highlight reconstruction (128.93 at strength 0), sharpening, or chroma denoise. The near/far ratio is 1.261 in the scene-linear dump and 1.282 after the tone curve, so the glow is veiling flare and demosaic mixing already present in the capture. At matched 1456px scale the relative glow is smaller than Windows Photo Viewer's render of the same ARW (1.144 against 1.172); only absolute foliage brightness differs, 72.67 against 39.09. The cause is curve shape, not level: matched-scale luminance percentiles are p05 29.4 / p25 57.9 / p50 92.1 / p75 138.1 / p95 210.5 for raw-autotune against p05 14.3 / p25 29.3 / p50 49.4 / p75 79.6 / p95 214.9 for Windows. The highlight end agrees within 2 percent while everything below sits about twice as high, which is a midtone and shadow contrast difference, not an offset. The automatic exposure is independently corroborated: the sidecar records target_median_ev -0.23569 against the embedded preview's center_weighted_key_display_ev -0.27450, agreeing to 0.04 EV, with key_score -0.28211 and tonal_class normal. Rev 1's exposure reading came from comparing parameters.exposure_ev +1.03732, the adjustment applied to the raw, against the preview key, a display-EV subject placement; they are different quantities and are not comparable.
        """
    supersedes [ @raw-autotune.evidence.backlit-foliage-glow-is-exposure-20260810/1 ]
}

